    "answer": {
      "short": "$f(x)=x^2$ is not one-to-one on $\\mathbb R$, so $g$ is not its inverse on that domain. Restrict $f$ to $[0,\\infty)$; then $g=f^{-1}$ and the range of $g$ is $[0,\\infty)$.",
      "worked": "$f(x)=x^2$ fails the horizontal line test on $\\mathbb R$, so it has no inverse function on that domain. Also,\n$$\\begin{align*}g(f(x))&=\\sqrt{x^2}\\\\g(f(x))&=|x|.\\end{align*}$$\nThis equals $x$ only when $x\\geq0$. Restricting the domain of $f$ to $[0,\\infty)$ makes $g(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ its inverse. The range of $g$ is then $[0,\\infty)$, matching the restricted domain of $f$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
